kubernetes is an open source container  orchestration tool which has gained  immense popularity over the last several  years  as more companies began to adopt  kubernetes there came to be a demand for  kubernetes to be provided as a managed  service  that's where google kubernetes engine  comes in i'm caslin fields and i'm a  developer advocate here at google cloud  where i focus on google kubernetes  engine or gke as well as open source  kubernetes and today we're going to take  a look at some gke essentials around the  different types of cluster modes  available through gke  [Music]  kubernetes is a platform for running  applications by using the kubernetes  software to create a cluster of machines  allowing you to run your applications  while abstracting the hardware that the  applications are running on  when running open source kubernetes  clusters from scratch there are a number  of components of the cluster itself  which you will need to understand  these include the control plane which  itself is made up of several components  as well as provisioning and management  of the underlying compute nodes  security and networking configuration of  the nodes as well as the cluster as a  whole  patching and upgrades of both the  cluster software and the compute nodes  that make up the cluster  monitoring or observability components  as well as management tools such as  policy configuration capabilities  scaling tools such as pod level and  cluster or node level auto scalers  and availability and reliability  capabilities and tools  some of these components are built into  kubernetes itself and some are essential  capabilities which may require  additional tooling  when running kubernetes from scratch you  will need to develop a level of  proficiency across all of these  categories and be prepared for the  maintenance configuration and  exploration running a do-it-yourself  cluster will require  google kubernetes engine or gke is a  managed kubernetes service from google  cloud it allows you to spin up a  kubernetes cluster quickly with many of  the more complex components of the  system being deployed and managed by  google  this way you can focus more on running  the applications that matter to you on  kubernetes rather than spending as much  time understanding kubernetes itself  there are two modes of operation in gke  each is designed to allow users  different levels of involvement with  kubernetes itself  both autopilot mode and standard mode  gke clusters take some of the management  overhead of kubernetes clusters off your  hands  standard mode offers a higher degree of  control  the kubernetes control plane and some  other considerations are handled behind  the scenes by google but you will still  need to manage the nodes of the  kubernetes cluster gke autopilot mode  provides an even more hands-off  experience with smart defaults and even  less management overhead gke autopilot  lets you have more time and resources to  spend on your own applications if you  want minimal cluster management overhead  in order to focus more of your time and  resources on the applications you need  to run you may prefer gke autopilot mode  autopilot mode gke clusters provision  infrastructure resources as needed for  kubernetes workloads this means that you  pay per pod or per workload rather than  per node  the nodes of the kubernetes cluster are  automatically provisioned and managed  which means that there should be no need  to dedicate time and resources to node  management and upgrades any  infrastructure auto scaling will be  handled automatically by the autopilot  mode cluster however you'll need to  configure any desired auto scaling at  the workload or pod level  if you've been using kubernetes for some  time and have developed skills and  processes that require greater control  and insight into the cluster you may  prefer standard mode  standard mode the control plane and some  aspects of monitoring and management are  handled for you  but the user still must manage the nodes  of the kubernetes cluster themselves  this means that in standard mode you'll  pay per node for the instance types that  you select  standard mode clusters will require you  to dedicate some time and resources to  node management and upgrades  for securing and maintaining your gke  standard mode clusters worker nodes be  sure to familiarize yourself with the  gke hardening guide which is available  in the google cloud documentation all  four dimensions of auto scaling are  available to standard mode clusters  but you'll have to manually configure  any desired auto scalers at both the  workload and infrastructure levels for  each cluster  as a managed kubernetes service google  kubernetes engine provides users with an  easier path to getting started running  their applications with kubernetes  between standard mode and autopilot mode  gke provides different levels of control  and flexibility depending on how  hands-on teams want to be with managing  their kubernetes clusters  with the concepts discussed in this  video we're confident you'll be on your  way to building a gke cluster that suits  your needs you can get started with gke  by visiting google cloud console and  checking out the links below and stay  tuned for our next videos where we dive  deeper into gke topics  [Music]  you
 

